  "fits": [
    {
      "fact": "Sized for small-to-medium tanks of roughly 40–60 cm.",
      "source": 1
    },
    {
      "fact": "One shop says the tank should be 40–50 cm long with walls at least 29 cm tall so the unit hangs properly.",
      "source": 0
    },
    {
      "fact": "One distributor recommends it only for tanks under 70 L.",
      "source": 5
    },
    {
      "fact": "May lack enough flow for large or heavily stocked tanks, which may need a second filter.",
      "source": 4
    },
    {
      "fact": "Some shops say it can skim the water surface to remove oil film; one says the skimmer attachment is sold separately.",
      "source": 2
    },
    {
      "fact": "Its compact body suits setups where there is little room behind the tank.",
      "source": 3
    }
  ],
  "usage": [
    {
      "fact": "Prime it first: fill the filter body with water before plugging it in.",
      "source": 0
    },
    {
      "fact": "Fill the intake until water comes out of the outlet, attach the tubes, hang it on the tank and switch on. Trapped air should clear and it should run quietly after about 5 minutes.",
      "source": 1
    },
    {
      "fact": "Use the knob to set the flow to suit the tank size and the fish.",
      "source": 0
    },
    {
      "fact": "The included sponge only filters debris, so add biological media such as ceramic rings or Matrix. Activated carbon can also be added.",
      "source": 1
    },
    {
      "fact": "Don't pack the media so tightly that water can't flow, and rinse the sponge regularly, because a clogged sponge can make the filter overflow.",
      "source": 1
    },
    {
      "fact": "Use only one layer of floss in the fine stage to prevent clogging. Because the chamber is small, high-quality media such as Matrix or Substrat Pro is recommended.",
      "source": 5
    },
    {
      "fact": "Rinse the sponge in old tank water, and replace it only when it starts falling apart.",
      "source": 6
    },
    {
      "fact": "Clean or replace media periodically following the manual.",
      "source": 4
    }
  ],
  "issues": [
    {
      "fact": "Running it dry, a jammed impeller, or physical damage are not covered by the 1-month warranty.",
      "source": 5
    },
    {
      "fact": "On the sister model HBL-803, a buyer said the filter sucked in air and was loud at first; tightening loose screws fixed it. Low flow was blamed on trapped air, which is common in this type of filter.",
      "source": 7
    },
    {
      "fact": "Shops disagree on the specs (4 vs 5 chambers, 5 W vs 6 W, recommended tank size), so check the box before buying.",
      "source": 0
    },
    {
      "fact": "The 801, 802 and 803 all list the same 6 W, 500 L/h rating. The bigger models mainly add body size and media space, not pump power.",
      "source": 6
    },
    {
      "fact": "Many Vietnamese shops list it as out of stock.",
      "source": 3
    }
  ],
